Key Definitions

A variable which stores the address of another variable is known as a pointer variable (or pointer).


a is a variable of int type → int type variables store integer data.
ip is a variable of int* type → int* type variables store integer addresses.

cint a;
a = 5;
int *ip;       // ip is a pointer to int
ip = &a;       // ip stores address of a
printf("%d", a);   // 5  (value)
printf("%d", &a);  // 500 (address — some value like 500)
printf("%d", ip);  // 500 (same address stored in ip)
printf("%d", *ip); // 5   (value at address ip points to)

For any variable, however many bytes are allocated — if we print the address, only the starting one byte address is printed.
